Package: worksuite-desktop
Architecture: all
Depends: worksuite-shell, worksuite-session, worksuite-branding,
 worksuite-user-services, worksuite-package-service, worksuite-device-agent,
 labwc, greetd, cage, gtkgreet | worksuite-greeter, gtklock, swayidle,
 xdg-desktop-portal, xdg-desktop-portal-wlr, xdg-desktop-portal-gtk, gnome-keyring,
 pipewire, pipewire-pulse, wireplumber, network-manager, bluez, udisks2, upower, cups, polkitd, pkexec,
 flatpak, fcitx5, apparmor, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: fwupd
Suggests: worksuite-office
Conflicts: openssh-server
Description: WorkSuite OS desktop (metapackage)
 Pulls in the WorkSuite session, shell, system services and the Debian
 desktop stack they rely on. No SSH server (WSOS-SEC-010). The WorkSuite web
 apps (Mail, Calendar, Chat, Drive, ...) come with worksuite-shell; WorkSuite
 Office (native editors) is published separately and added to the image when
 its .deb is provided. The native panel, Wayland bridge and archive keyring
 packages join when their sources exist.

Package: worksuite-shell
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends: libc6, libgtk-3-0t64 | libgtk-3-0, libgtk-3-bin, desktop-file-utils, libnss3, libasound2t64 | libasound2, libgbm1, libdrm2, libxshmfence1, ${misc:Depends}
Description: WorkSuite shell (Electron launcher, dashboard, Settings, App Center)
 The React/Electron desktop shell of WorkSuite OS: greeter preview, launcher,
 desktop, Files, App Center, Settings, recovery launcher and installer UI,
 with a bundled Electron runtime. Also provides worksuite-node, the Node
 runtime the WorkSuite services run on, and worksuite-app, which opens each
 WorkSuite web app (Mail, Calendar, Chat, Drive, Tasks, Projects, CRM, Meet,
 PDF Studio) in its own window, with their menu entries, icons and the
 worksuite:// link handler.

Package: worksuite-session
Architecture: all
Depends: labwc, systemd, dbus-user-session, ${misc:Depends}
Description: WorkSuite Wayland session file, systemd user units and launcher
 Session entry for the display manager, the worksuite-session launcher and the
 systemd user units bound to worksuite-session.target, plus Labwc and portal
 configuration.

Package: worksuite-user-services
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends: worksuite-shell (= ${binary:Version}), gnome-keyring, libsecret-tools, xdg-utils, ${misc:Depends}
Description: WorkSuite user services (catalog, files, credentials broker, sync)
 Per-user services: the offline-first sync service (local SQLite store and
 outbox) and the credentials broker (OneAuth sign-in, keyring-held refresh
 tokens).

Package: worksuite-package-service
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends: worksuite-shell (= ${binary:Version}), apt, flatpak, polkitd, dbus, ${misc:Depends}
Description: WorkSuite system package service (D-Bus + polkit)
 Root service on the system bus that installs, removes and updates APT and
 Flatpak software for the shell and the device agent, behind polkit, one job
 at a time.

Package: worksuite-device-agent
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends: worksuite-shell (= ${binary:Version}), cryptsetup, dbus, systemd, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: tpm2-tools
Description: WorkSuite device agent (enrollment, policy, signed remote commands)
 Root agent for organization-managed devices: enrollment, signed policy,
 heartbeat, signed remote commands and remote wipe.

Package: calamares-settings-worksuite
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends: calamares (>= 3.3), cryptsetup, python3, ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: WorkSuite OS installer configuration for Calamares
 Calamares settings, branding and two WorkSuite modules for the live ISO:
 a typed disk-name confirmation before erasing (WSOS-INS-003) and a LUKS2
 recovery key added during install and shown once (WSOS-INS-004).

Package: worksuite-branding
Architecture: all
Depends: plymouth, plymouth-label, grub-common, ${misc:Depends}
Description: WorkSuite OS boot splash, GRUB theme, icons and wallpaper
 The WorkSuite logo as the Plymouth boot splash (including the disk unlock
 prompt), the GRUB menu theme with a quiet boot command line, the application
 icon in the hicolor theme and the default desktop wallpaper.
